      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "Wider-area botulinum toxin (BT) injection with the dosage depending on specific pathology is a promising approach for the treatment of hemifacial spasm (HFS), resulting in effective and long-lasting control of HFS with fewer side-effects."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "The aim of this study was to develop a BT injection strategy that minimizes complications by considering the causes, duration, and electrophysiologic features of the disease, as well as the patient's age."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "From July 2011 to July 2015, 26 patients were included in the strategy. The mean age was 61.8\u2009±\u200914.6. In the case of essential HFS, 2 units/injection site was the standard dosage. If a patient was aged over 60 and had a reduced electromyogram (EMG) amplitude, the dosage was reduced by 0.5 units/site. In the case of post-paralytic and tumor-induced HFS, 1.5 units/site was the standard dosage. All cases were managed by alternating injections of Botox and Dysport with no physical therapy."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Most HFS patients treated with the injection strategy had complete remission of HFS within 1 week. The average number of BT injection sites was 22.6\u2009±\u20096.7. The mean total BT dosage on the affected side was 28.6\u2009±\u20094.9 units. The mean duration of BT efficacy was 28.6\u2009±\u20097.7 weeks."
        }
      ]},
